---
/**
 * ISO 6346 CHECK DIGIT CALCULATOR AND VALIDATOR
 * =============================================
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE IS FOR, because it changes how it is built. This is a linkable asset. Resource
 * pages, reference lists and editors link to free calculators, and they do not link to sellers.
 * So the page has to stand up as a public utility on its own terms, which means three deliberate
 * departures from the other commercial pages here:
 *
 *   1. pageType="tool", so the FloatingSidebar lead-capture aside does NOT render. A calculator
 *      with a sales panel following it down the screen is a funnel with a widget bolted on, and
 *      nobody links to one of those. The schema prop is still kind: 'guide', because the layout
 *      takes pageType and schema as independent inputs and the Article plus FAQPage graph is
 *      exactly right for this page. Same schema shape as /container-rent-vs-buy-calculator/.
 *   2. No prices anywhere, and no pricing.ts import at all. There is nothing on this page a price
 *      would belong to.
 *   3. One quote link, in body prose, and no full width call to action band.
 *
 * NO SECOND COPY OF THE ARITHMETIC. Everything numeric on this page, the reference letter table,
 * the worked example, and the running calculator, comes from src/lib/iso6346.ts. The static parts
 * are computed here at build time; the interactive part imports the same module into a bundled
 * <script> (precedent: src/pages/quote/index.astro imports src/lib/attribution.ts the same way).
 * Two copies of an arithmetic rule is one copy plus a future disagreement.
 *
 * DEGRADES WITH JS OFF. Everything except the input field is server rendered: the four part
 * anatomy, the full letter table, the ten row worked example with its sum and remainder, all the
 * prose, and the FAQ. The tool block swaps its heading and hides its form when the wrapper never
 * gets .has-js, and the paper method stays visible either way.
 *
 * COLOUR. Cobalt hero and ink working field, the same treatment as the rent vs buy calculator, so
 * the two tools read as one family rather than two unrelated pages.
 *
 * Route: /iso-6346-check-digit-calculator/. Flat and top level, matching
 * /container-rent-vs-buy-calculator/ rather than the nested /size/calculator/, because
 * /size/calculator/ sits under the size guide it belongs to and this tool has no parent guide.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  LETTER_VALUES,
  WEIGHTS,
  CATEGORY_LETTERS,
  analyzeContainerNumber,
} from '../../lib/iso6346';

// Static page. No prerender directive, matching /container-rent-vs-buy-calculator/ and /cost/:
// output is 'hybrid', which prerenders a plain page like this one by default.

const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'ISO 6346 Check Digit Calculator' }];

const BIC_CALCULATOR = 'https://www.bic-code.org/check-digit-calculator/';

// The letter table, straight off the module, so the page cannot print a value the tests do not
// hold. Split into three columns of nine, eight and nine for the reference card.
const letterRows = Object.entries(LETTER_VALUES).map(([letter, value]) => ({ letter, value }));
const letterColumns = [letterRows.slice(0, 9), letterRows.slice(9, 18), letterRows.slice(18)];

// The worked example. KEIU900079 with a check digit of 8, the same number the blog post walks,
// so a reader who arrives from that post sees the arithmetic they were just taught.
const example = analyzeContainerNumber('KEIU9000798');
const exampleNumber = example.normalized;
const exampleQuotient = Math.floor((example.sum as number) / 11);

const categoryList = Object.entries(CATEGORY_LETTERS);

// Single source for the page FAQ. These strings ARE the FAQPage JSON-LD answers, and the first
// three also render into the QuickFacts card, which slices them off the front of this array.
const faqs: { q: string; a: string }[] = [
  {
    q: 'What is a container check digit?',
    a: 'It is the eleventh character of a shipping container number, the single digit painted '
      + 'inside a box at the end of the ID. It is not chosen by anybody. It is computed from the '
      + 'ten characters in front of it by a formula defined in ISO 6346, so the number carries its '
      + 'own proof that it was written down correctly. It works the way the last digit of a credit '
      + 'card number works: if one character gets mistyped or misread, the arithmetic stops '
      + 'agreeing and the error shows up before it travels any further.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you calculate the check digit on a shipping container?',
    a: 'Turn each of the first ten characters into a number. Digits keep their face value. Letters '
      + 'use a fixed table that starts at A equals 10 and skips every multiple of 11, so A is 10, '
      + 'B is 12, K is 21, L is 23, U is 32 and Z is 38. Multiply each value by a doubling weight '
      + 'running 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on up to 512, left to right. Add the ten products, divide the '
      + 'total by 11, and the remainder is the check digit. There is one exception: a remainder of '
      + '10 becomes a check digit of 0, because a check digit has to fit in one character.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does it mean if a container number fails the check digit?',
    a: 'Almost always that a character was misread rather than that anything is wrong with the '
      + 'container. Read the number off the box again before you conclude anything, because 8 and '
      + 'B, 0 and O, 1 and I, and 5 and S all look alike on a worn stencil at dusk. If the number '
      + 'still fails after a careful re-read, ask the seller where their number came from. A '
      + 'transcription error somewhere in a chain of paperwork is the ordinary explanation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a valid check digit mean the container is legitimate?',
    a: 'No. It means the eleven characters are arithmetically consistent with each other, and that '
      + 'is all it means. Any correctly formed number passes, including one somebody made up on '
      + 'the spot, because the formula has no idea whether a container exists. A matching check '
      + 'digit tells you nothing about who owns the unit, what condition it is in, whether it has '
      + 'ever been examined, or whether the number stencilled on the door was ever the number that '
      + 'belonged to that door. Treat it as a spell check on a string of characters, not as '
      + 'provenance.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this the official BIC check digit calculator?',
    a: 'No. The Bureau International des Containers, the body that maintains the owner code '
      + 'registry, publishes its own free calculator at bic-code.org, and that one is the official '
      + 'one. This page is not affiliated with BIC, is not endorsed by BIC, and carries no '
      + 'authority of its own. It implements the arithmetic ISO 6346 defines and shows every step '
      + 'of it, which is the one thing it does that the official calculator does not. If you want '
      + 'the authoritative answer, use theirs. If you want to see why the answer is what it is, or '
      + 'you need to check a number somebody has already given you, use this.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the fourth letter in a container number?',
    a: 'It is the equipment category identifier, and ISO 6346 defines exactly three: U for a '
      + 'freight container, J for detachable freight container related equipment, and Z for '
      + 'trailers and chassis. On a storage container or any ordinary dry box it is U, effectively '
      + 'without exception. If you have read something else off a stencil, the likeliest answer is '
      + 'that it is a worn U rather than a category you have not met, so re-read the character '
      + 'before you go looking for an explanation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where is the container number written on a container?',
    a: 'In several places, and they should all agree. It is stencilled on both cargo doors at the '
      + 'right hand end, on the roof so it can be read from a crane or a gantry, and usually on '
      + 'both side walls near the corner. The check digit is the digit inside the small box at the '
      + 'end. Directly underneath the ID on most boxes is a separate four character size and type '
      + 'code, something like 45G1, which describes the container rather than identifying it and is '
      + 'not part of the eleven characters this page checks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you look up who owns a container from its number?',
    a: 'You can look up the three letter owner code, which is the registered prefix of a fleet, in '
      + 'the public BIC Code Register. That tells you which company holds that prefix today, which '
      + 'is not necessarily the company that owned the unit when it was built and is not the '
      + 'company that sold it to you. There is no public record of who owns one individual '
      + 'container. Used containers change hands repeatedly and nobody publishes the chain, so ask '
      + 'the seller to show you their own paperwork rather than expecting a database to do it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this calculator send my container number anywhere?',
    a: 'No. The arithmetic runs entirely in your browser, in about a dozen lines of JavaScript that '
      + 'arrive with the page. There is no request to any server when you type, nothing is stored, '
      + 'nothing is logged, and there is nothing to sign up for. Once the page has loaded it works '
      + 'with no connection at all, which is useful in a container yard, where that is often the '
      + 'situation.',
  },
];

// QuickFacts cells are atomic surfaces with no room for a hedge, so every one of them is a plain
// fact about the standard or about the tool. None makes a claim about any container.
const specs = [
  { k: 'What this checks', v: 'The ISO 6346 check digit on a container number' },
  { k: 'Number format', v: '4 letters plus 6 digits plus 1 check digit' },
  { k: 'The arithmetic', v: 'Weighted sum, modulo 11, with 10 folding to 0' },
  { k: 'Runs', v: 'In your browser. No network call, nothing stored' },
  { k: 'Official calculator', v: 'BIC publishes its own at bic-code.org' },
  { k: 'What it cannot tell you', v: 'Ownership, condition, or examination history' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  pageType="tool"
  title="ISO 6346 Container Check Digit Calculator | Steel Box Direct"
  description="Free ISO 6346 check digit calculator. Validate an 11-character shipping container number or work out the check digit for 4 letters and 6 digits, with every step of the arithmetic shown."
  datePublished="2026-09-02"
  dateModified="2026-09-02"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  schema={{
    kind: 'guide',
    title: 'ISO 6346 Container Number Check Digit Calculator and Validator',
    specs,
    faqs,
  }}
>

  <!-- ============ 1. HERO ============ -->
  <div class="cd-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="cd-crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / ISO 6346 Check Digit Calculator</p>
      <div class="cd-eyebrow">
        <span class="idx">&sect; TOOL</span>
        <span class="nm">ISO 6346, Annex A</span>
        <span class="tg">Check Digit</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="cd-headline">ISO 6346 check digit<br /><em>calculator and <span class="nb">validator</span>.</em></h1>

      <div class="cd-statement">
        <span class="m cd-statement-k">What this does</span>
        <p>Paste eleven characters and find out whether they add up.</p>
        <p class="cd-statement-body">
          Every shipping container number ends in a digit that is computed from the ten characters
          in front of it. Enter all eleven and this tells you whether the last one is right. Enter
          just the first ten, four letters and six digits, and it works out what the eleventh should
          be. Either way it shows you the whole sum, row by row, so you can follow it or check it by
          hand.
        </p>
        <p class="cd-statement-body">
          It is free, there is nothing to sign up for, and nothing you type leaves your browser.
          Read the section on what a matching check digit does not prove before you rely on the
          answer for anything that matters, because it proves less than most people assume.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="cd-hero-jump">
        <li><a href="#tool">Check a number</a></li>
        <li><a href="#anatomy">What the eleven characters are</a></li>
        <li><a href="#method">How the arithmetic works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limits">What a valid check digit does not prove</a></li>
        <li><a href="#official">The official BIC calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">Common questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ 2. THE TOOL ============ -->
  <section class="cd-tool" id="tool" aria-labelledby="tool-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cd-sh cd-sh-dark">
        <span class="m">Check a number</span>
        <h2 class="cd-h2" id="tool-h">Enter a container number</h2>
        <p class="cd-sh-sub">
          Eleven characters to check one, ten to work one out. Case does not matter, and spaces,
          hyphens and full stops are ignored, so a number copied off a bill of lading works exactly
          as well as one typed off a door. Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is saved.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="calc" id="cd-calc-wrap">
        <div class="calc-sheet">
          <form class="calc-form" id="cd-form" novalidate>
            <div class="calc-field">
              <label for="cd-input">Container number</label>
              <input
                type="text"
                id="cd-input"
                name="cd-input"
                placeholder="CSQU3054383"
                maxlength="24"
                autocomplete="off"
                autocapitalize="characters"
                spellcheck="false"
                aria-describedby="cd-help"
              />
              <p class="calc-help" id="cd-help">
                For example CSQU3054383, or csqu 305438 3, or CSQU305438 on its own to have the
                check digit worked out.
              </p>
            </div>
          </form>

          <div class="calc-actions">
            <button type="submit" form="cd-form" class="btn">Check it</button>
            <button type="button" class="calc-reset" id="cd-reset">Clear</button>
          </div>

          <p class="calc-hint">
            The field starts empty on purpose, and the answer updates as you type. If you would
            rather not type a real number in, the worked example further down this page runs the
            same arithmetic on a number we picked.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- The answer. Ships in its empty state and is written into by the script. -->
        <div class="calc-out" id="cd-out" role="status" aria-live="polite">
          <div class="calc-out-grid">
            <div class="calc-out-a">
              <span class="m calc-out-k">The verdict</span>
              <p class="calc-big" id="cd-verdict">Waiting for a number</p>
            </div>
            <div class="calc-out-b">
              <p class="calc-explain" id="cd-explain">
                Type or paste a container number above. If it is eleven characters, this says
                whether the check digit agrees with the other ten. If it is ten, this gives you the
                digit that belongs on the end.
              </p>

              <div class="calc-parts" id="cd-parts" hidden>
                <div><span class="m">Owner code</span><b id="cd-owner">-</b></div>
                <div><span class="m">Category</span><b id="cd-category">-</b></div>
                <div><span class="m">Serial</span><b id="cd-serial">-</b></div>
                <div><span class="m">Check digit</span><b id="cd-digit">-</b></div>
              </div>

              <p class="calc-warn" id="cd-warn" hidden></p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The working. Hidden until there is something to show. -->
        <div class="calc-work" id="cd-work" hidden>
          <h3 class="calc-work-h">The arithmetic, row by row</h3>
          <p class="calc-work-sub" id="cd-work-sub"></p>
          <div class="tbl-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Check digit arithmetic" tabindex="0">
            <table class="cd-table cd-table-work">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Position</span></th>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Character</span></th>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Value</span></th>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Weight</span></th>
                  <th scope="col" class="rt-hi"><span class="rt-title">Product</span></th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody id="cd-work-rows"></tbody>
              <tfoot id="cd-work-foot"></tfoot>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Shown only when the wrapper never gets .has-js. -->
        <div class="calc-nojs">
          <strong>JavaScript is off, so here is the same arithmetic on paper.</strong>
          <ol>
            <li>Write out the first ten characters of the number. Leave the eleventh, the check digit, to one side.</li>
            <li>Give each character a value. A digit is worth its face value. A letter comes from the table below.</li>
            <li>Multiply the first character by 1, the second by 2, the third by 4, and keep doubling: 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512.</li>
            <li>Add the ten products together.</li>
            <li>Divide the total by 11 and keep the remainder. That is the check digit. If the remainder comes to 10, the check digit is 0.</li>
            <li>Compare it to the digit in the box at the end of the number. They should match.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The full letter table and a ten row worked example are both further down this page, and
            neither needs JavaScript. The only thing the script adds is doing it for you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 3. ANATOMY ============ -->
  <section class="cd-anatomy" id="anatomy" aria-labelledby="anatomy-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cd-sh">
        <span class="m">What you are looking at</span>
        <h2 class="cd-h2" id="anatomy-h">Eleven characters, four parts</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="anat-grid">
        <div class="anat-body">
          <p>
            An ISO 6346 container number is eleven characters long and it always breaks into the
            same four parts. Reading left to right: three letters of owner code, one category
            letter, six digits of serial number, and one check digit. It is stencilled on both cargo
            doors, on the roof so it can be read from above, and usually on the side walls too, and
            every copy of it should say the same thing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The check digit is the one inside the small box at the end. That box is not decoration.
            It marks the character that is computed rather than assigned, and it is the reason a
            container number can catch its own typos.
          </p>
          <p>
            Directly under the ID on most containers there is a second, shorter code, four
            characters like 45G1. That is the size and type code, it describes the box rather than
            identifying it, and it is not part of the eleven characters this page checks. Our post on
            <a href="/blog/how-to-read-container-id-number-iso-6346/">how to read a container ID number</a>
            decodes both codes side by side on a real example.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="anat-card">
          <span class="m anat-card-k">Worked from the module</span>
          <p class="anat-num">
            <span class="anat-owner">{exampleNumber.slice(0, 3)}</span><span
            class="anat-cat">{exampleNumber.slice(3, 4)}</span> <span
            class="anat-serial">{exampleNumber.slice(4, 10)}</span> <span
            class="anat-check">{exampleNumber.slice(10)}</span>
          </p>
          <dl class="anat-key">
            <div><dt><span class="sw sw-owner"></span>Owner code</dt><dd>{example.ownerCode}, the registered prefix of a fleet</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="sw sw-cat"></span>Category</dt><dd>{example.categoryLetter}, {(example.categoryMeaning ?? '').toLowerCase()}</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="sw sw-serial"></span>Serial</dt><dd>{example.serial}, this one unit</dd></div>
            <div><dt><span class="sw sw-check"></span>Check digit</dt><dd>{example.checkDigit}, computed from the other ten</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="anat-cats">
        <h3>The fourth character</h3>
        <p>
          The category letter is the one part of the number people get wrong most often, because
          there are only three legal values and one of them accounts for almost everything.
        </p>
        <ul class="cat-list">
          {categoryList.map(([letter, meaning]) => (
            <li><b>{letter}</b><span>{meaning}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="anat-cats-note">
          On a dry storage box it is U, effectively always. If you have read anything else off a
          stencil, a worn U is far likelier than a category you have not met before, so re-read the
          character before you go looking for an explanation. The tool above still runs the
          arithmetic on any letter, because the formula is defined for all of them, and flags the
          character rather than refusing to answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 4. THE METHOD ============ -->
  <section class="cd-method" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cd-sh cd-sh-dark">
        <span class="m">How it works</span>
        <h2 class="cd-h2" id="method-h">The arithmetic, and why it is shaped like that</h2>
        <p class="cd-sh-sub">
          ISO 6346 defines the check digit in an annex, and the whole method is three steps long.
          Everything below can be done on the back of an envelope, which is the point: a check that
          needs a database is no use to somebody standing in a yard.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="method-steps">
        <div class="method-step">
          <span class="m">Step 1</span>
          <h3>Turn each character into a number</h3>
          <p>
            Digits are worth their face value. Letters come from a fixed table that starts at A
            equals 10 and then skips every multiple of 11. That skip is the only clever part of the
            whole scheme. A value divisible by the modulus is invisible to the modulus, so if any
            letter were worth 11, 22 or 33 it could be swapped for another letter without changing
            the answer, and the check would silently stop catching that swap.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-step">
          <span class="m">Step 2</span>
          <h3>Multiply by a doubling weight</h3>
          <p>
            The weights run {WEIGHTS.join(', ')}, left to right, which is 2 to the power of the
            position. Because every weight is different, moving a character to a different position
            changes its contribution, which is how the check catches a transposition. Two digits
            swapped over is one of the two commonest mistakes people make copying a number down, and
            a scheme that just added the characters up would miss all of them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method-step">
          <span class="m">Step 3</span>
          <h3>Sum, divide by 11, keep the remainder</h3>
          <p>
            Add the ten products. Divide the total by 11. The remainder is the check digit. There is
            one exception and it is the only special case in the standard: a remainder of 10 would
            not fit in a single character, so it is written as 0. That means 0 in the box has two
            different causes, a remainder of 0 and a remainder of 10, and the tool above shows you
            which one you got.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="method-tables">
        <div class="method-table-block">
          <h3 class="method-table-h">The letter table</h3>
          <p class="method-table-sub">
            All twenty six values, generated from the skip rule rather than typed in, so the rule
            above is the only thing this page depends on.
          </p>
          <div class="letter-cols">
            {letterColumns.map((col) => (
              <table class="cd-table cd-table-letters">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Letter</span></th><th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Value</span></th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {col.map((row) => (
                    <tr><th scope="row">{row.letter}</th><td>{row.value}</td></tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="method-table-note">
            The gaps are at 11, 22 and 33. That is why L jumps from K at 21 straight to 23, and why
            V jumps from U at 32 to 34.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="method-table-block">
          <h3 class="method-table-h">A worked example: {exampleNumber}</h3>
          <p class="method-table-sub">
            The same number our ISO 6346 post walks through, run here by the same code that runs the
            calculator above. It needs no JavaScript, because it was computed when this page was
            built.
          </p>
          <div class="tbl-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Worked example arithmetic" tabindex="0">
            <table class="cd-table cd-table-work">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Position</span></th>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Character</span></th>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Value</span></th>
                  <th scope="col"><span class="rt-title">Weight</span></th>
                  <th scope="col" class="rt-hi"><span class="rt-title">Product</span></th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {example.steps.map((s) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{s.position}</th>
                    <td class="cd-char">{s.char}</td>
                    <td>{s.value}</td>
                    <td>{s.weight}</td>
                    <td class="is-hi">{s.product.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
              <tfoot>
                <tr class="cd-sum">
                  <th scope="row" colspan="4">Sum of the ten products</th>
                  <td class="is-hi">{(example.sum as number).toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="cd-mod">
                  <th scope="row" colspan="4">
                    {(example.sum as number).toLocaleString('en-US')} divided by 11 is {exampleQuotient.toLocaleString('en-US')}, remainder
                  </th>
                  <td class="is-hi">{example.remainder}</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="cd-result">
                  <th scope="row" colspan="4">Check digit, and the digit in the box on the door</th>
                  <td class="is-hi">{example.checkDigit}</td>
                </tr>
              </tfoot>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="method-table-note">
            The remainder is {example.remainder}, the boxed digit on the door is {example.givenCheckDigit},
            and they agree. The number was written down correctly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ 5. THE LIMITS ============ -->
  <section class="cd-limits" id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cd-sh">
        <span class="m">Read this before you rely on it</span>
        <h2 class="cd-h2" id="limits-h">What a matching check digit does not prove</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="limits-intro">
        This is the part of the page worth your time. A check digit that agrees is a good sign about
        one narrow thing: the eleven characters are consistent with each other, so whoever wrote the
        number down probably wrote it down right. That is the entire claim. It is a spell check on a
        string of characters. It is not evidence about a container, and the list below is not
        hedging, it is the actual scope of the arithmetic.
      </p>

      <p class="cd-table-caption" id="limits-tbl-cap">
        Five things people read into a matching check digit, and what it actually says.
        <span class="cd-table-hint">Scroll the table sideways on a small screen</span>
      </p>
      <div class="tbl-scroll" role="region" aria-labelledby="limits-tbl-cap" tabindex="0">
        <table class="cd-table cd-table-limits">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" class="rt-rowhead"><span class="rt-title">What people assume</span></th>
              <th scope="col" class="rt-hi"><span class="rt-title">What the arithmetic actually supports</span></th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The container exists</th>
              <td class="is-hi">
                It does not show that. The formula runs on a string of characters and has no idea
                whether there is any steel behind them. Any correctly formed number passes,
                including one invented on the spot, and inventing one that passes takes about a
                minute with this page open.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The seller owns it</th>
              <td class="is-hi">
                Ownership of one individual unit is not published anywhere. The three letter owner
                code is a fleet prefix, and the BIC Code Register tells you which company holds that
                prefix today, which may not be who owned the box when it was built and is not who is
                selling it to you. Ask the seller for their own paperwork.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The container is in good condition</th>
              <td class="is-hi">
                A matching number says nothing about rust, the floor, the door seals, or whether the
                doors still line up. Those are questions for photographs and a walk around the box
                in daylight. Our <a href="/condition/">condition guide</a> covers what the grades
                actually mean and what to look at.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">It has passed an examination</th>
              <td class="is-hi">
                Separate question, separate marking, and one the number cannot answer. Examination
                status lives on the CSC plate and its decals, not in the ID, and no free public
                lookup returns a specific container's current status. Our
                <a href="/container-certification-guide/">container certification guide</a> explains
                what the plate does and does not record, and our
                <a href="/find-a-container-inspector/">inspector finder</a> lists certified
                inspectors by state if you want a qualified person to look at it.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">This number belongs to this container</th>
              <td class="is-hi">
                The arithmetic checks the number, not the pairing. A stencil can be repainted and a
                plate can be replaced, both for entirely ordinary reasons. What a passing check digit
                tells you is that the characters in front of you are internally consistent, whoever
                painted them there.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="limits-notes">
        <div class="limits-note">
          <strong>What we are, and what we are not</strong>
          <p>
            Steel Box Direct sells used Wind and Water Tight shipping containers. We built this page
            because we already had the arithmetic written down and a free tool that shows its working
            seemed more useful than a fourth page about ourselves. We do not perform inspections, we
            do not issue certificates, and we are not a registry. This tool checks arithmetic on a
            number, and that is the whole of what it does.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="limits-note">
          <strong>If the number does not check out</strong>
          <p>
            Re-read it off the box before you conclude anything at all. On a worn stencil at dusk, 8
            and B, 0 and O, 1 and I, and 5 and S are all easy to confuse, and a misread character is
            far and away the commonest cause of a failure. If it still fails after a careful second
            look, ask the seller where their number came from. A typo somewhere in a chain of
            paperwork is the ordinary explanation, and it is worth resolving rather than ignoring.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

